full_judgment:
S. H. Md. Nurul Huda Jaigirdar, J: The instant restoration application has been filed at the instance of the plaintiffs-petitioners for restoration of the Civil Revision No. 4824 of 2014 to its original file and number after re-calling the order of discharging for default dated 05.05.2016 passed by this court.

2. Mr. Khalequzzaman, the learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the petitioners submits that the Civil Revision came up in the daily cause list on 05.05.2016 as Item No. 35 for hearing, but due to personal difficulties of the learned Advocate for the petitioners Md. Shahimuddin could not appear before the Hon'ble Court in time for hearing when the Civil Revision was called on for hearing, as a result of that no one appeared before the Court and accordingly the Rule was discharged for default. He further submits that he had no laches to appear before the Court in time but for unavoidable circumstances it happened and therefore, it is required to be restored.

3. Mr. Md. Khalilur Rahman, the learned Advocate appearing for the opposite parties by filing Counter Affidavit against the application for restoration filed by the plaintiffs-petitioners on 05.06.2016 as well as Supplementary Affidavit dated 13.06.2016 and 14.06.2016 contended that:

(a) before replying to the facts stated in the main restoration petition dated 05.06.2016, supplementary affidavit in support thereof dated 13.06.2016 and 14.06.2016, this opposite parties upon whose application the impugned order of abatement dated 05.02.2014 was passed are praying to state the facts happened during pendency of the Civil Revision No. 4824 of 2014 for proper, effectual and complete adjudication of the present restoration application;

(b) the Ministry of Land of the Government of Bangladesh and the Land Reform Board, under whom the Court of Wards, Bhawal Raj Estate is being managed and administered the retainable property of the left out wards of that Bhawal Raj Estate having already taken decision not to claim over the Projabili property cited in the Projabili property Gazette and issued Memorandum No. বি, আর, ঢাকা-৮১/২০০৩/৩২৪ dated 10.07.2014 under the signature of the Manager of Court of Wards, Bhawal Raj Estate, Memorandum No. 3100100001 0421671032115-987 dated 27.10. 2015 issued under the signature of Assistant Secretary of the Ministry of Land, Memorandum No. 311 001000010461581019112-70 dated 02.02.2016 issued under the signature of the Deputy Secretary, Ministry of Land, and lastly Memorandum No. 03107110221311 0010011 2014-67(17)/3 dated 22.03.2016 issued under the signature of the Principal Secretary of the Prime Minister's office and as such, the present petition for restoration of the Civil Revision filed against the order of abatement dated 05.02.2014 of the learned Joint District Judge and Paribesh Adalat, Dhaka in Title Suit No. 1932 of 2008 as the property remained in the list of Projabili property; so, the present restoration petition is nothing but a vexations and harassing one;

(c) with regard to the restoration application the learned Advocate contended that the grounds stated in the main restoration petition and the supplementary affidavit being contradictory to one another is false and made out for the purpose of this restoration petition.

In support of his contention the leaned Advocate referred to the case of Sanatannessa Bewa —vs- Haipatullah Sarkar and others, reported in 41 DLR (AD) P-105.

04. We have carefully gone through the restoration application and supplementary affidavit thereto filed by the plaintiffs-petitioners and counter affidavit by opposite parties and the materials on record viz the present application for restoration of the Civil Revision filed against the impugned order of abatement dated 05.02.2014.

05. On perusal of the restoration application along with the supplementary affidavit and the Counter Affidavits, it appears that the petitioners explanation for default are not satisfactorily explained and on consideration of the impugned abatement order dated 05.02.2014 passed by the Joint District Judge and Paribesh Adalat, Dhaka in Title Suit No. 1932 of 2008 wherein, it further appears that the abatement order dated 05.02.2014 challenged by the plaintiffs-petitioners is only question of law that Title Suit No. 1932 of 2008 is maintainable in the eye of law, so before considering the restoration application we may consider the application under Section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure on merit for ends of justice:

(a) on an application under Section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure, a Rule was issued calling upon the opposite party Nos. 1 to 383 to show cause as to why the impugned judgment and order No.140 dated 05.02.2014 passed by the Special Judge (Joint District and Sessions Judge) of Paribesh Adalat, Dhaka in Title Suit No. 1932 of 2008 abating the suit should not be set aside and/or such other or further order or orders passed as to this court may seem fit and proper;

(b) petitioners as plaintiffs instituted Title Suit No. 18 of 2001 (re-numbered as Title Suit No. 1932 of 2008) before the Subordinate Judge, 3rd Court, Dhaka for declaration of title and recovery of khas possession after removing the obstacle against the defendants opposite parties;

(c) the relevant facts described by the plaintiffs-petitioners in the revisional application under Section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure are that the suit-land of C.S. Khatian No. 63 and 66 of correspondent to C.S. Plot Nos. 81 &82 along with contagious land of Mouza Sukrabad within Police Station formerely Kerenigoaj at present Dhanmondi, District Dhaka, Originally belongs to Kumar Rabindra Narayan Roy Chowdhury, Kumar Ramendra Narayan Roy Chowdhury & Kumar Ronendra Narayan Roy Chowdhury all sons of Kumars Raja Rajendra Narayan Roy Chowdhury the Rajas of Bhawal. After the death of aforesaid three Kumars Rajas, the successors i.e the wives of aforesaid three Rajas declared disqualified in pursuance of the section 6(e) of The Court of Wards Act. 1879 and the entire properties of Bhawal Raj Estate have been vested upon to the court of Wards BhawaL Raj Estate in 1911 and 1912. Thereafter, the court of wards Bhawal Raj Estate have been in full management and control of the properties including the aforesaid properties since 1911 and that of during the C.S. operation the aforesaid properties were recorded correctly in the name of the court of wards Bhawal Raj Estate; the classification of the suit land was pond and bank of a pond, which were usually used for public utilizations i.e for bathing and abulation etc. and court of Wards has been owning and enjoying the same by pisci culature; the plaintiff petitioners further case is that the court of wards Bhawal Raj Estate did never settle the land with anybody in any manner; at the time of S.A. operation, the concern officer, officials of the survey office, recorded the schedule properties along with adjoining properties, the name of some unauthorized occupants in lieu of the court of wards Bhawal Raj Estate and also during the R. S. Operation, the official of survey department illegally and fictitiously recorded the name of some unauthorized occupants in lieu of the name of court of Wards Bhawal Raj Estate; the plaintiff petitioners came to know to that effect at first on 14.11. 2000; in the instant suit the defendants Nos. 1 to '70 along with their persons on 17.11.2000 with a view to illegally grabbing the suit land and illegally squatting there from 18. 11. 2000 by earth-filing and by changing the mature and character of the suit-land started constructing building of high rise Apartment; on being informed the plaintiffs/petitioners on 18.11.2000, the plaintiffs/Assistant Manager at the instance of the plaintiffs forbade the Defendants not to earth filing of the suit land; but the defendant on their persons disobeying the direction of the plaintiffs officer, while started constructing building, the plaintiffs by his Lawyer served legal notices on 20.11.2000; in this manner for recording title of the plaintiff/ petitioners has been clouded; hence, the plaintiffs -petitioners have been compelled to institute the Title Suit No. 18 of 2001 in the court of Joint District Judge 3rd Court, Dhaka . on 30.11.2000 for declaration of title with a relief for recovery of khas possession against the Defendant opposite-party Nos. 01 to 70 and the proforma-defendant-Opposite party Nos. 71 to 73; subsequently the said suit was transferred to the learned Special Judge ( Joint District & Sessions Judge) of Poribesh Adalat, Dhaka and renumbered as Title Suit No. 1932 of 2008;

(d) the Defendant opposite party Nos. 02, 47 & 48 appeared in the suit on different dates and contested the same by filing separate written statements, denying all the material allegations contending inter alia that previously the Suit land was vested to the Government and subsequently suit land was partitioned and Swarnamoyee Devi owned as Zaminder and the suit land was registered in D Registered as new Touzi No. 16495; some of the defendants opposite parties including the defendant/ opposite party Nos. 02, 47 & 48 also claiming that the predecessor-in-interest got the suit land by permanent settlement from the Swarnamoyee Devi; thereafter suit land with other contagious land was auctioned for non-payment of lease money; the predecessors of the defendant/ opposite parties Moulavi Abul Fazal Mohammad and others acquired 16 Bigha 01 Kattha 03 Chatak land including the suit land of Touzi No. 16495 of the Dhaka Collectorate Mouja Sukrabad by way of purchase in Certificate Case No. 318 in the year 1935-1936 for valuable consideration and obtained Khas possession thereof; the aforesaid touzi No. 16495 was belong to Swarnamoyee Divi; the original Zamindari touzi being No. 5846 comprising of 6 (six) C.S. Khatians being Nos. 63, 66, 60, 62, 64 and 67 in which total area of land in plot Nos. 81 & 82 was 2.19 acres; the said Zamindari Touzi was later on divided and separate Touzi being No. 16495 of Swarnamoyee Devi was created; defendant- opposite party Nos. 47 & 48 further claiming that the property of Touzi No. 5846 of Mouza Sukrabad Comprising of 11 C.S. plots including C.S. Plot Nos. 81 and 82 (which includes the suit land to the extent of 1.03 acres of land) was acquired by the predecessor in interest of the Defendant-opposite party Nos. 47 & 48 by way of purchase in certificate case No. 318 of 1935-1936 and the said predecessor in interest Moulovi Abul Fazal Mohammad obtaining actual physical possession of the properties belonging to the predecessor in interest of the defendant opposite party nos. 47 & 48 were Requisitioned under Section 3 of the East Bengal ( Emergency) Requisition of property Act. 1948 (E.B Act XIII of 1948) by notification; thereafter the said land was Requisitioned by Government letter being No. 2620 of dated 15.08.1956 in favour of the said predecessor in interest Moulvi Abul Fazal and his brothers of the defendant Opposite party Nos. 47 & 48; the predecessors of the Defendants- opposite parties were taken award money as Awardee; later on, the concerning office of the Government has issued a latter and directed to deposit development cost of Rs. 8,620 to the Government which were taken earlier; the said Abul Fazal Mohammad in compliance with the direction of the Government deposited the said amount to the

State Bank of Pakistan, Dhaka by Treasury Challan No. 38, dated 23.08.1956 and the said fact was intimated to the Government by letter dated 25.08.1956; defendant-opposite party Nos. 47 & 48 further claiming that the said predecessor in interest of the Defendant Opposite party Nos. 47 & 48 while owning and possessing the said properties including the said property after the whole sale acquisition under East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act. 1950 during S.A. Operation the said property including the suit property was recorded in S.A. Khatian No. 284 being plot No. 169 in the name of said Haji Abul Fazal Mohammad and his brothers as co-sharers as per their respective shares and was finally published without any objection whatsoever from any corner including the defendants/ opposite parties or their predecessor in office; thereafter the heirs and successors of the said Haji Abul Fazal Mohammad and his co sharers, while they were owning and possessing the said property including the suit property and the same during R.S. operation it was correctly recorded in their names in R.S. Khatian No.991 being R.S. plot No. 680 and it 681 and was finally published ; defendant- opposite party Nos. 47 & 48 further claiming that the Defendant opposite party Nos. 47 & 48 are the rightful owners in possession of the suit property to the extent of 784 Ajutangsha i.e 4 Kattha 12 chatak well demarcated land with specific boundaries appertaining to S.A. Khatian No. 284 of plot No. 169 and R.S. Khatian No. 991 of plot No. 680,681 by way of purchase from its rightful owners in possession, who by way of inheritance and thereafter by amicable partition dated 15.06 1990 acquired the same; defendant opposite party Nos. 47 & 48 after purchase of the said property mutated their names in the Government Tahsil office and paid rents to the Government and also they after obtaining sanction of plan from the RAM( an investing huge amount of money constructed multistoried, puccas building in the said land and mutated their names in the Tax Assessment Register of the Dhaka City Corporation and obtained New Holding being No. 19 Dhanmondi R/A, Road No. 13 and paid Municipal Taxes to the City Corporation regularly. The defendants/ opposite parties also brought Electric, Gas, Was a connection and paid bill and charges in their names regularly; during recent Mohanagar Jarip their names were recorded up to the Tasdik stage but after final publication it was found that their properties has been wrongly recorded in the names of government of the People’s Republic of Bangladesh, Land Reforms Board, Ministry of Land. The Defendant opposite party Nos. 47 & 48 shall take necessary legal steps in the appropriate forum against the said wrong recording in the Mohanagar Jarip. It need be mentioned here that the plaintiff’s petitioners filed several objection cases under section 30 & Appeals under Section 31 of the East Bengal State acquisition and Tenancy Rules, 1955 being Appeal case Nos. 31651, 31657, 31658, 31659, 31660, 31661, 31662, 31663, 31664, 31665, 31666, 31667, 31668, 31669, 31670 and 31671, 31672, 31673, 31674, 31675, 3176, 31677, 31678, 31679, 31680, 31681, 31682, 31683, 31684 against several defendants regarding the suit property and those objection cases under section 30 were rejected and Appeals under Section 31 were also rejected on contest and the recording in the name of the land owners defendants/ Opposite parties were upheld; in the manner as stated the defendants opposite parties are the rightful owners in possession of their self acquired properties including the suit property by declaring and exercising every act of ownerships and possession openly, continuously, uninterruptedly and adversely against all including the defendants/opposite parties for long more than 60 years and the statuary period of limitation; the plaintiffs petitioners/ defendants have or had no semblance of any right, title, interest and possession over the property belonging to the defendants/ opposite parties including the suit property; the plaintiffs petitioners with malafide object of illegal gain or corrupt income generating filed the present vexatious suit against the defendants/ opposite parties only of making and harassing the defendant/ opposite parties by creating unwarranted pressure of litigation; the plaintiffs petitioners have no locus-standi to file the present suit against the defendant opposite parties nor they are entitled to any legal character or legal right to bring such a suit against the defendant opposite parties. The plaintiffs petitioners having not disclosed any cause of action or specific point of time of entering into possession by the defendants/opposite parties and also the suit land being totally vague, indefinite, unidentifiable and unspecified and as such the plaintiffs petitioners are not in law and equity are entitled to any decree as prayed for and as such the suit should be dismissed with compensatory costs in favour of the defendants.

(e) defendant opposite parties Nos. 06 & 07 appeared in the suit and contested the same by filling separate written statements denying all the material allegations contending inter alia that the suit land with contagious land of C.S. khatian Nos. 62 to 69 of correspondent to C.S. Plot Nos. 81 & 82 of Mouza Sukrabad within the police station formerly Keranigonj at present Dhanmondi, District Dhaka, originally belongs to Swarnomoyee Devi, Kripamoyee Devi and others. That Moulana Abdus Sobhan took permanent settlement of 16 Bigha 01 Kattha 03 Chatak of land including the suit land from the Swarnamoyee Devi and others and obtained Khas possession thereof. the nature of suit property were /was khal, doba, nala etc. that defendant opposite party Nos. 06 &07 acquired the part of the suit land by way of more than separate registered deeds from the successors of the Moulovi Abdus Sobhan and they were owning and possessing the part of suit land continuously, uninterruptedly and adversely for long more than 60 years; the plaintiffs have no locus-standi to file the present suit against the defendants nor they are entitled to any legal character of legal right to bring such a suit against the defendants;

(f) the learned Court below after perusing the plaint and written statements framing issues on 22.02.2005 in 05 points which are as follows:-

(১) বর্তমান আকারে ও প্রকারে অত্র মোকদ্দমা সচল কিনা?

(২) অত্র মোকদ্দমার মূল্যায়ন সঠিক কিনা?

(৩) নালিশী সম্পত্তিতে বাদীপক্ষের স্বার্থ দখল বিদ্যমান আছে কিনা?

(৪) বাদী প্রার্থীত মতে ডিক্রি পাইবার অধিকারী কিনা?

(৫) বাদী অন্যান্য কোন প্রতিকার পাইবার অধিকারী কিনা?

(g) after framing aforesaid issues the suit was fixed for preemptory hearing on different dates and P.W. 1 started deposition from 28.05.2005; when the contested defendants/ opposite parties ends their cross examination of the P.W. 1 thereafter the defendants opposite party Nos. 74-438 filed several separate petition for adding parties on different dates, which were allowed after hearing on different dates;

(h) on 13.02.2013 the defendant opposite party filed an application under Order 14 rule 02 and under section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure for framing 03 (three) new issues on question point of law and dispose of the suit, which were mentioned in para Nos. 05, 06, & 07 of the application Annexure "F" of the Revisional application; the plaintiffs petitioners filed written objection on 12.06.2013 against the application dated 13.02.2013 and denying the materials contention of the application contending inter alia that the application in not maintainable according to law and information and the applicants i.e. The defendant Nos. 6,7,13,16-30, 28, 29, 34, 35, 48, 65-68, 69 & 74-448 have no locus-standi to filed the aforesaid application dated 13.02,.2013;

(i) the defendant Nos. 6,7,13,16-20,28,29,34,35,48,65-68,69 &74-448, further filed an application before the learned court below under section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure dated 06.10.2013 and the same was fixed for hearing on 25.11.2013;

(j) the defendant Nos. 6,7,13,16-30,28,29,34,35,48,65-68,69 & 74-448 further filed an application without serving copy to the plaintiffs petitioners before the learned court below out of date dated 08,10.2013, fixing the date of hearing within 01/02 days before the date of 25.11.2013 of the application dated 06.10.2013 which was filed earlier. But the learned Court below passed and order on 08.10.2013 stating therein that the application was fixed for hearing on 31.10.2013 after serving the copy of the application dated 08.10.2013 in presence of both the parties; on 12.11.2013 the learned Court below heard both the applications dated 08.10.2013 and 06.10.2013 of the defendant Nos. 6,7,13,16-30,28, 29, 34, 35, 48, 65-68, 69 &74-448 and fixed on 25.11.2013 for order; on the date fixed on 25.11,2013 the plaintiffs petitioners filed written objection against the application dated 06.10.2013. the learned court below fixed the next date on 16.01.2014 for hearing the petition is presence of both the parties; on that date i.e. 16.01.2014 the defendant Nos. 6,7,13,16-30, 28, 29, 34, 35, 48, 65-68, 69 & 74-448 further filed another application under section 151 of the Code of Civil procedure for setting aside the order dated 08.09.2013 and for passing order of two applications dated 13.02.2013 under order 14 rule 2 red with section 151 of the code of civil procedure for passing preliminary issues on point of law and another application  dated 06.1.2013 for abating the suit without serving the copy of the plaintiffs petitioners; the learned court below filed the next date on 23.01.2014 for hearing on condition that the copy be served upon the plaintiff petitioners; on 23.01.2014 the learned advocate of the plaintiff petitioners filed time petition. After hearing the time petition the learned court below fixing the next date on 28.01.2014 for hearing; the plaintiff petitioners on that day dated 28.01.2014 filed written objection against the application dated 16.01.2014 and after hearing both the parties the learned court below was fixed 05.02.2014 for order and on 05.02.2014 the learned court below passed the impugned judgment and order considering the evidence, the case of the plaintiff petitioners, setting aside the earlier order dated 08.09.2013 and allowing the application dated 13.02.2013 and allowing another application dated 06.10.2013 for abating the suit;

(k) Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the judgment and order N.140 dated 05.02.2014 allowing the application dated 06.10.2013 for abating the suit, the plaintiffs-petitioners preferred this revisional application and obtained the Rule.

(1) The petitioners of the revisional application under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure have taken the rule on the following grounds:

(i) the impugned judgment and order is mixed question of law and facts, it should not be decided without framing issues and proper trial and learned Subordinate without framing issues summarily disposed of the case which is an error of law resulting an error occasioning failure of justice;

(ii) the learned court below erred in law facts and equity occasioning failure of justice in not considering that the court of Wards Bhawal Raj Estate never settled the suit properties to anybody in any manner;

(iii) the learned Court below erred in law in not considering that Swaranmoyee Devi, Kripamoyee Devi and others were not owner of the suit-properties along with contagious properties of Bhawal Raj Estate and they have no locus-standi to transfer or settle the suit-properties to anybody in any manner whatsoever;

(iv) the learned Court below erred in law grossly in not considering that the Court of Wards Bhawal Raj Estate is fully authorized for maintenance, control and overall management of the suit-properties;

(v) the learned Court below erred in law in not considering that the except prior consent and approval of the Court of Wards Bhawal Raj Estate none is authorized to settle the Suit-properties;

(vi) the learned Court below erred in law in not considering that the defendant-opposite parties did not file any documents in support of their claims and for that the defendant-opposite parties have no locus-standi to file an application for abating the suit or any other applications;

(vii) the learned Court below erred in law in not considering that the learned court below on 08.09.2013 passed the order stating therein the petition dated 13.02.2013 will be decided after disposal of the Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal No. 486 of 2013 and letter on before disposal of the said Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal No. 486 of 2013 the learned Court below arbitrarily and fancifully set-aside the earlier order dated 08.09.2013 and passed the impugned judgment and order without framing issues as per order 14 rule-02 and without proper trial abated the suit which is not in accordance with law;

6. Mr. Md. Khalilur Rahman, the learned Advocate for the opposite parties takes us through the revisional application under Section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure, plaint and written statement of the parties, counter affidavit and supplementary affidavit on behalf of the opposite parties in support of the counter affidavit dated 15.06.2016 and connected materials on record and contended that:

(a) the Ministry of Land of the Government of Bangladesh and the Land Reform Board, under whom the Court of Wards, Bhawal Raj Estate is being managed and administered the retainable property of the left out wards of that Bhawal Raj Estate having already taken decision not to claim over the Projabili Property cited in the projabili property gazette and issued Memorandum No. রি,আর,ঢাকা-৮১/২০০৩/৩২৪ dated. 10.07. 2014 under the signature of the Manager of Court of Wards, Bhawal Raj Estate, Memorandum No. 31100100001 0421671 032115-987 dated 27.10.2015 issued under the signature of Assistant Secretary of the Ministry of land, Memorandum No. 3110010000104615 810191 12-70 dated 02.02.2016 issued under the signature of the Deputy Secretary, Ministry of Land and lastly Memorandum No. 0310711022131100100112014-67(17)/3 dated. 22.03.2016 issued under the signature of the Principal Secretary of the Prime Minister's office and as such, the present petition for restoration of the Civil Revision filed against the order of abatement dated 05.02.2014 of the Learned Joint District Judge and Paribesh Adalat, Dhaka in Title Suit No. 1932 of 2008 as the property remained in the list of Projabili Property. So, the present restoration petition is nothing but a vexations and harassing one.

7. Defendants opposite parties filed an abatement application before the trial court by swearing affidavit on 02.10.2013 under Section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure which reads as follows:

“জিলা ঢাকার বিজ্ঞ যুগ্ম জেলা জজ ও পরিবেশ আদালত, ঢাকা।

দেওয়ানী মোকদ্দমা নং ১৯৩২/২০০৮

কোর্ট অব ওয়াডর্স গং

----- বাদী।

বনাম

জাহাংগীর মোহাম্মদ আদেল গং

----- বিবাদী।

দেওয়ানী কার্যবিধি আইনের ১৫১ ধারার ক্ষমতাবলে পি,ও ৯০/৭২ এর নির্দেশিত মতে অত্র মোকদ্দমাটি দরখাস্তকারীগণের ক্ষেত্রে এবেট বা খারিজ করার প্রার্থনায় ৬,৭,১৩,১৬-২০, ২৮,২৯,৩৪,৩৫,৩৮,৬৫-৬৮,৬৯ এবং ৭৪-৪৪৮ নং বিবাদী দরখাস্তকারীগনের পক্ষে।

বিনীত নিবেদন এই যেঃ-

১। নালিশী “শুক্রাবাদ” মৌজাস্থিত জে,এল নং ২৬০ এর সি, এস ৬৩ ও ৬৬ নং খতিয়ানের রেকর্ডকৃত ভূমিতে তৎকালীন মালিক “কুমার রবীন্দ্র নারায়ন রায় চৌধুরী ওয়ারিশন গং পক্ষে কোর্ট অব ওয়াডর্সঞ্জ কর্তৃক প্রজাবিলি করা হইয়াছিল বিধায় এবং উক্ত দুইটি খতিয়ানের সম্পত্তি বাদে উক্ত জমিদারের উক্ত মৌজা ও জে, এল নম্বর ২৬০ ভুক্ত আর কোন সম্পত্তি উক্ত কুমার রবীন্দ্র নারায়ন রায় চৌধুরীর মালিকানা এবং দখল না থাকায় সরকার কর্তৃক উক্ত জমিদারের প্রজাবিলি সম্পত্তিতে ১৯৫০ সনের রাষ্ট্রীয় অধিগ্রহন ও প্রজাস্বত্ব আইনের বিধান মতে জমিদারের খাজনা গ্রহনের অধিকার হুকুম দখল করানার্থে এবং প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তিতে প্রজাদের মালিকানা ঘোষনা করনার্থে গত ২৪-০৩-৫২ ইং তারিখে রাষ্ট্রীয় অধিগ্রহন ও প্রজাস্বত্ব আইনের ৩ ধারার বিধান মতে প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তির যে গেজেট প্রকাশ করেন সেই গেজেটটিতে উক্ত দুইটি সি, এস খতিয়ানের সংশ্লিষ্ট মৌজা “শুক্রাবাদ” ও সংশ্লিষ্ট জে, এল নং “২৬০” ৪৮৮ পৃষ্ঠায় উল্লেখিত হয়।

২। সরকার শুধুমাত্র মৌজার নাম ও জে, এল নম্বর উল্লেখে ঐরুপ গেজেটে প্রকাশ করেই ক্ষান্ত হন নাই বরং উক্ত গেজেট কার্করীক্রমে যখন স্বস্ব প্রজাদের দখলীয় সম্পত্তির বিষয়ে পরবর্তী এস, এ রেকর্ড প্রস্তুত ও প্রকাশ করেন তখন উক্ত দুইটি সি, এস খতিয়ানে উল্লেখিত ভূমি এস এ যথাক্রমে ১৬৯, ১৭২ ও ১৭৩ নং দাগে প্রজাই স্বত্ত্বে মালিকানা অর্জনের ভিত্তি হিসাবে সংশ্লিষ্ট এস, এ ২৮৪ নং খতিয়ানের  মন্তব্যের কলামে রাষ্ট্রীয় অধিগ্রহন ও প্রজাস্বত্ব আইনের “২৪(১) জ ধারার বিধানমতে মালিক মর্মে উল্লেখ ছাড়াও উক্ত রেকর্ডমতে প্রজাই স্বত্বে মালিকগনের স্ব স্ব মালিকানা স্বীকারে চুড়ান্তভাবে প্রকাশিত এস, এ রেকর্ডমতে খাজনাদি গ্রহণ করেন। ইহার পর সরকার পরবর্তীতে যখন আর, এস, রেকর্ড প্রস্তুত ও প্রকাশ করেন তখনও সরকার উক্ত প্রজাই স্বত্ত্বে এস, এ রেকডীয় মালিকগনের মাধ্যমে ওয়ারিশ সুত্রে মালিক ও দখলদার সরাফ উদ্দিন গং এর নামে যথাযথ ও সঠিকভাবে আর, এস ৯৯১ নং খতিয়ান চুড়ান্তভাবে প্রস্তুত ও প্রকাশ করেন ও উক্ত চুড়ান্তভাবে প্রকাশিত রেকর্ডের ভিত্তিতে খাজনাদি গ্রহন করেন।

৩। ইহার পর উক্ত আর এস রেকডীয় মালিক সরাফ উদ্দিন গং তাহাদের মালিক দখলীয় সম্পত্তিতে নিজে ও ডেভলপারের মাধ্যমে বহুতলা এ্যাপার্টমেন্ট বিল্ডিং নির্মানে নির্মিত এ্যাপার্টমেন্ট বিল্ডিং নির্মানে নির্মিত এ্যাপার্টমেন্টগুলি ও উহাদের সহিত সংযুক্ত অবিভক্ত ও অবিভাজ্য ভুমি ও অন্যান্য সুযোগ সুবিধাদি অত্র দরখাস্তকারীগনের বরাবরে ভিন্ন ভিন্ রেজিষ্ট্রি কবালা দলিলমূলে সাফ বিক্রয়ে নিঃস্বত্ববান হইলে দরখাস্তকারীগন ক্রয়সুত্রে যার যার ক্রয়কৃত ফ্ল্যাটে ও উহার সহিত সংযুক্ত অবিভক্ত ও অবিভাজ্য ভূমি ও কমন সুযোগ সুবিধা দিতে বৈধ মালিক দখলকার থাকেন ও আছেন।

৪। উক্তরুপে সরকার কর্তৃক রাষ্ট্রীয় অধিগ্রহণ ও প্রজাস্বত্ব আইনের ৩(১) ধারার বিধানমতে প্রকাশিত প্রজাবিলি সম্পত্তির গেজেট মুলে এবং ১৯৭৯ ইং সনের কোর্ট অব ওয়ার্ডস আইনের ৮(১) ধারার বিধান মতে প্রজাইস্বত্বে এস,এ রেকডীয় মালিকগন তাহাদের প্রজাইস্বত্বে অর্জিত সম্পত্তির মালিকানা ও দখলে থাকাকালে যেন পূর্বের জমিদারগন কর্তৃক বাধাগ্রস্ত হইতে থাকেন তখন সরকার সকল বাধাবিঘ্ন দুরীকরনার্থে এবং প্রজাইস্বত্বে মালিকদের মালিকানা ও দখল করনার্থে পি,ও ৯০/৭২ জারী করতঃ সরকারের আইন প্রয়োগের মাধ্যমে প্রজাইস্বত্বে মালিক ও দখলদারগনের মালিকানাকে চ্যালেঞ্জ করিয়া যাহাতে পূর্বের জমিদার কোর্ট অব ওয়ার্ডস ও অন্যান্য জমিদারগন কোন প্রকার মামলা মোকদ্দমা বা অন্য কোন প্রকার আইনগত পদক্ষেপ গ্রহন করিতে না পারে তৎমর্মে উক্ত পি,ও ৯০/৭২ দ্বারা বারিত করার পাশা-পাশি সুস্পষ্টভাবে উল্লেখ করেন যে, ১৯৫০ সনের ৩ ধারা কর্য্যক্রমে প্রজাইসত্বে মালিকগনের মালিকানা চ্যালেন্বঞ্চ করিয়া পূর্বের জমিদার কর্তৃক রুজুকৃত মোকদ্দমাগুলি মোকদ্দমাগুলি তাৎক্ষনিক ভাবে এবট বা খারিজ হইবে। আইনের উপরোক্ত বিষয়টি মহামান্য সুপ্রীম কোর্টের আপীল বিভাগ ১৪ এম, এল, আরতে ৪০১ পৃষ্ঠায় উল্লেখিত কোর্ট অব ওয়ার্ডস বনাম রমিসা খানম গং এর মোকদ্দমার রায়ে এবং পরবর্তীতে উক্ত রায় অনুসরনে মহামান্য হাইকোর্ট বিভাগ রীট মোকদ্দমার ১৭০৫৬/১২ ও ১৭২১৬/১২ তে গত ১৫-০৪-২০১৩ ইং তারিখে প্রদত্ত রায়ে কোর্টের আপীল বিভাগের রায়ে সংশ্লিষ্ট অংশটুকু নিম্নরুপঃ-

(L) "The learned Additional Attorney General while making his submission in favour of the leave petitioner found it difficult to support the leave petitioner, since confronted with the gazette notifications dated 24.03.1952 showing acquisition of rent receiving interest under Section 3(1) of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act in respect of the lands covered by Monza Raza Bazar under J.L. No. 2 and the gazette notification dated 29.02.1956 showing acquisition and vesting of the lands, in respect of which the rent receiving interest have been acquired under Section 43(2), 46 C an 46E(3) of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act,1950 in favour of the Government."

(M) The Hon'ble Appellate Division further held:

"With the publication of the Gazette notifications dated 24.03.1952 and 29.02.1956 the leave petitioner ceased to have any right, title and interest and possession in the case lands."

(N)Thereafter the court also held: —

"Thus we are of the view that the leave petitioner had no locus-standi to challenge the draft Mahanagar Survey Khatian as prepaid in the names of the respective writ petitioners, at least after publication of the gazette notifications dated 24.03.1952 and 29.02.1956."

অন্যদিকে মহামান্য হাইকোর্ট বিভাগ কর্তৃক ১৭০৫৬/১২ এবং ১৭২১৬/১২ নং রীট মোকদ্দমাতে প্রদত্ত রায়ের সংশ্লিষ্ঠ অংশটুকু নিম্নরুপঃ

"The Court of Wards and land Reform Board, cannot, under any circumstances, claim ownership over the projabili property of the previous Zamindar, Kumar Rabindra Narayan Ray Chowdhury and as such cannot issue the impugned two Memoranda, specifically when they were parties in the aforesaid cases before the Appellate Division. Such interference by the respondents are nothing but harassment to the previous "Projas" who has become owners of the respective properties by operation of law.

We are confronted with unnecessary and uncalled for litigations like the present one in spite of setting the self same issue connecting with "projabili property" by the Apex Court in its different decisions reported in 33 DLR (S.C.) 13 and 53, 27 DLR (S.C.) 109 and lastly in the case reported in 14 MLR (A.D.) 401.

It has already been settled earlier in the aforesaid cases holding that the "projabili property" can never be claimed by the previous zamindars represented by the court of Wards. The court of Wards though legally bound to follow the said decision according to Article 112 of the constitution but time and again they are harassing the people claiming title over the " projabili Property" either by issuing Memorandum or by other ways and means and thereby also wasting the courts valuable time. So it is high time to caution of court of Wards. Bhawal Raj Estate and also the land Reform Board to abstain from creating such situation on frivolous issues and there by pushing the public in the court. Thus they are cautioned not to harass anybody specifically the previous "Projas" or their successors in interests any more, claiming title over the "Projabili Property" as cited in the "Projabili Property" Gazette published on 24.03.1952. If in further, any such illegal and uncalled for claim is sought for by the court of Wards or if any litigation is continuing or started afresh because of court of Wards action by claiming the title over the said "Projabili Property" by the court of Wards and the land Reform Board, then they would be liable to be dealt with for contempt of Court, apart from such litigations being abated under P.O. 90 of 1972.

৫। যেহেতু অত্র মোকদ্দমার বাদী কোর্ট অব ওয়ার্ডস, ভাওয়াল রাজ এস্টেট উহার আরজিতে অত্র বিবাদীগনের পূর্ববর্তী ও বিবাদীগনের নামীয় এস, এ ও আর, এস রেকর্ড প্রস্তত ও প্রকাম হওয়া স্বীকার করেনে এবং উক্ত রেকর্ডীতে উল্লেখিত মালিক দখলকারগনের দখল স্বীকারে অত্র মোকদ্দমায় উহার খাস দখল প্রার্থনা করিয়াছেন এবং যেহেতু নালিশী সম্পত্তির সংশ্লিষ্ট মৌজা ও জে, এল নং প্রজাবিলি সম্পত্তি হিসাবে উল্লেখিত আছে সেইহেতু পি, ও, ৯০/৭২ এর বিধানমতে অত্র মোকদ্দমাটি এবেট বা খারিজ করার যথাবিহীত সিদ্ধান্ত দেওয়া একান্ত আবশ্যক। নতুবা অত্র বিবাদী দরখাস্তকারীপক্ষের অপুরনীয় ক্ষতির কারন হইবে।

অতএব, উপরোক্ত অবস্থা ও ঘটনার প্রেক্ষিতে অত্র বিবাদী দরখাস্তকারীপক্ষের বিনীত প্রার্থনা এই যে পি, ও ৯০/৭২ এর বিধানমতে মোকদ্দমাটি এবেট মর্মে সিদ্ধান্ত দিয়া মোকদ্দমাটি খারিজ করিবার যথাবিহীত আদেশ দানে সুবিচার করিতে মর্জি হয়। ইতি তাং----ইং।

8. On detailed consideration of abatement application under Section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure Annexure-"H" to the revisional application, application dated 13.02.2013 under Order 14 rule 02 and Section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure Annexure-"F" to the revisional application, written objection against the application for abatement of the suit Annexure-"I" to the revisional application and connected materials on record the learned Special Judge passed the elaborate impugned order the relevant portion of the order is "quoted" below:

“১৪০----০৫-০২-২০১৪—আজ ০৮-০৯-২০১৩ ইং তারিখের আদেশ রদ ও রহিত করে ইতোপূর্বে বিবাদীপক্ষের দাখিলকৃত দরখাস্তের বিষয়ে শুনানী অন্তে আদেশের জন্য ধার্য্য আছে। বাদীপক্ষ এবং ৬-৭, ১৩,২০, ২৮-২৯, ৩৪-৩৫, ৩৮, ৬৫-৬৯, ৭৪-৪৪৮ নং বিবাদীপক্ষ হাজিরা দাখিল করেছে। বিবাদীপক্ষের দেওয়ানী কার্যবিধির ১৫১ ধারার বিধান মোতাবেক দাখিলকৃত মোকদ্দমাটি এবেট/খারিজের দরখাস্তের বিরুদ্ধে বাদীপক্ষ লিখিত আপত্তি দাখিল করেছে এবং বাদীপক্ষ, বিবাদীপক্ষ থেকে দেওয়ানী কার্যবিধির ১৫১ ধারার বিধান মোতাবেক গত ০৮-০৯-২০১৩ ইং তারিখের আদেশ রদ ও রহিত করে উপরোক্ত দরখাস্তগুলো দ্রুত নিস্পত্তির প্রর্থনার দাখিলকৃত দরখাস্তের বিরুদ্ধে ও লিখিত আপত্তি দাখিল করেছেন। নথি শুনানীর জন্য পেশ করা হলো।---

---নথি পর্যালোচনায় দেখা যায় যে, এই মোকদ্দমায় নালিশী ভুমি প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তি কিনা এ বিষয়ে পক্ষদের মধ্যে বিরোধ রয়েছে এবং উভয় পক্ষের স্বীকৃত মতে, এই মোকদ্দমার কোন বিষয়ে পক্ষগন মহামান্য উচ্চ আদালতের স্বরণাপন্ন হয়নি। তাই দ্রুত নিষ্পত্তি ও ন্যায় বিচারের স্বার্থে ঢাকার তৃতীয় যুগ্ম জেলা জজ আদালতের দেওয়ানী ৭৫/০৭ নং মোকদ্দমা থেকে উদ্ভুত সিভিল পিটিশন ফর লীভ টু আপীল ৪৮৬/১৩ এর আদেশ প্রাপ্ত না হলেও এই মোকদ্দমা নিস্পত্তিতে আইনত কোন বাধা নেই। তাই সার্বিক পর্যালোচনায় ন্যায় বিচারের স্বার্থে এই আদালতের গত ০৮-০৯-১৩ তারিখের আদেশ রদ ও রহিত পূর্বক এ মোকদ্দমাটিতে নালিশী সম্পত্তি প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তি কিনা এবং বর্তমান পর্যায়ে মোকদ্দমাটি এবেট/খারিজ যোগ্য কিনা এ বিষয়ে আইনগত সিদ্ধান্ত গ্রহনের নিমিত্তে নথি আদেশের জন্য গ্রহণ করা হলো।

উল্লেখিত বিবাদীপক্ষ নালিশী ভুমি প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তি গণ্যে মোকদ্দমাটি এবেট/খারিজ করার সমর্থন যুক্তি প্রদর্শন করেন যে, তফসিল বর্নিত নালিশী সম্পত্তি তৎকালীন জমিদার “কুমার রবীন্দ্র নারায়ন রায় চৌধুরীর ওয়ারিশ সূর্যবালা দেবী গং পক্ষ কোর্ট অব ওয়ার্ডস ভাওয়াল রাজ এস্টেট” এর প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তি হওয়ার কারনে উহা সরকার কর্তৃক গত ২৪-০৩-১৯৫২ ইং তারিখে ১৯৫০ সনের রাষ্ট্রীয় অধিগ্রহন ও প্রজাস্বত্ব আইনের ৩ ধারার বিধান মতে প্রকাশিত প্রজাবিলি সম্পত্তির যে গেজেট প্রকাশ করা হয় সেই গেজেট এর ৪৮৮ পৃষ্ঠায় নালিশী সম্পত্তির সংশ্লিষ্ট মৌজা সি, এস, শুক্রাবাদ ও জে, এল নং ২৬০ উল্লেখ করা হয় এবং উহা কার্যকরী ক্রমে প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তিতে পূর্বের জমিদারদের নাম ও ঠিকানা উল্লেখে যে ক্ষতিপূরন প্রদান গেজেট সরকার গত ২৯-০২-১৯৫৬ ইং তারিখে প্রকাশ করেন সেই গেজেট নালিশী সম্পত্তির তৎকালীন জমিদার কুমার রবীন্দ্র নারায়ন রায় চৌধুরী ওয়ারিশ গং পক্ষে কোর্ট অব ওয়ার্ডস ভাওয়াল রাজ এস্টেট এর নাম ৩১ নং ক্রমিক উল্লেখ করা আছে। এর নালিশী সম্পত্তির সংশ্লিষ্ট উক্ত মৌজা শুক্রবাদ ও জে, এল নং ২৬০ এর প্রজাবিলি সম্পত্তির বিষয়ে সরকার প্রজাদেরকে মালিক হিসাবে উল্লেখ করে যে ২৮৪ নং এস, এ খতিয়ান চুড়ান্তভাবে প্রস্তত ও প্রকাশ করেন সেই এস, এ খতিয়ানে মালিকের ‘ক’ কলামে দরখাস্তকারীগনের পূর্ববর্তী মাওলানা সরফুদ্দিন আহম্মেদ গং এর নাম এবং মালিকানা অর্জনের ভিত্তি হিসাবে মন্তব্যের কলামে র্ঠূ এ্যাক্ট এর ২৪/১ ধারা উল্লেখিত আছে। পরবর্তীতে আর, এস, রেকর্ড ও পূর্বের রেকর্ডের ধারাবাহিকতায় তাদের নাম হয়েছে। অত্র মোকদ্দমায় আরজিতে বাদীপক্ষ উক্ত খতিয়ান গুলার কথা স্বীকারে অত্র মোকদ্দমাতে চুড়ান্ত ভাবে প্রস্তত ও প্রকাশিত উক্ত দুইটি রেকর্ডের বিরুদ্ধ প্রতিকার প্রার্থনা করেন। উল্লেখ্য শুনানী কালে দরখাস্তকারী বিবাদীপক্ষের বিজ্ঞ কৌশুলী যুক্তি উপস্থাপন করেন যে, যে সকল মৌজা ও জে, এল নম্বর ভুক্ত সম্পত্তির সম্পূর্ন ভুমি প্রজাবিলিকৃত ছিল সেই সকল সম্পত্তির বিষয়ে শুধুমাত্র জে, এল নং ও মৌজার নাম উল্লেখে প্ৰজাবিলি সম্পত্তির গেজেট প্রকাশিত হয় ৷ আর যেসকল মৌজা ও জে, এল, নং ভুওন্ সম্পত্তির মধ্যেশুধুমাএ কতিপয় খতিয়ান তুমি প্রজাবিলিকৃত ছিলসেই সকল ভূমির ক্ষেএে জে, এল, নং মৌজা ওখতিয়ান নং উল্লেখ প্রজাবিলি গেজেট প্রকাশিত হয় ৷শুধু নালিশী তুমি সংঞান্ত নয়, অন্যান্য ভূমির বেলায়ও খতিয়ান ব্যতিত শুধুমাত্র মৌজা ও জে,এল নম্বর গেজেটে উল্লেখ করা হয়েছে৷ যেহেতুনালিশী ভূমি প্রজাবিলিকৃত তুমি, তাই মহামান্যসুপ্রীম কোর্টের আপীল বিভাগের রায়ের আলোকেবিবাদীপক্ষ এ মোকদ্দমাটি এবেট/খারিজের প্রার্থনা করেন৷

উভয়পক্ষের বিজ্ঞ কৌশুলীৱ বক্তব্যশুনিলাম এবং নথি পর্যালোচনা করলাম ৷ বিবাদীপক্ষেরদাখিলকৃত প্ৰজাবিলি সম্পত্তির গেজেটের ৪৮৮ নংপৃষ্ঠায় ১ নং কলামে জমিদার কুমার রবীন্দ্র নারায়নরায় চৌধুরীর ওয়ারিশ সূর্যবালা দেবী গং পক্ষে কোর্টঅব ওয়ার্ডস ভাওয়াল রাজ এষ্টেট হিসাবে নালিশীসম্পত্তি সংশ্লিষ্ট সি, এস, শুএগবাদ মৌজা এবং সি,এস, জে, এল নং ২৬০ হিসাবে উল্লেখ আছে এবংসরকার কর্তৃক গত ২৯-০২-১৯৫৬ ইং তারিখেপ্রকাশিত ক্ষতিপূরন প্রদান গেজেট ৩১ নং ক্রমিকনালিশী সম্পত্তি জমিদার কোর্ট অব ওয়ার্ডস ভাওয়ালরাজ এষ্টেটের এর নাম উল্লেখ আছে৷ উক্তক্ষতিপূরন গেজেট প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তির বিষয়ের[ষ্টীয় অধিগ্রহন ও প্রজাস্বতৃ আইন, ১৯৫০ এর ৩ধারার বিধান অনুযায়ী প্ৰজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তিরক্ষতিপুরন জমিদারদের বরাবরে প্রদান করা হয়েছে ৷তাছাড়া সরকার পূর্বের প্রজাগনকে মালিক উল্লেখকরে নালিশী সম্পত্তি সংএান্ত এস, এ… ২৮ ৪ নংখতিয়ানে মন্তব্য কলামে মালিকানার ভিত্তি হিসাবেপ্ৰজাস্বভূ মালিকানা অজর্মুনর বিষয় ২৪/১ ধারা মতেউল্লেখ আছে৷ এরই ধারাবাহিকতায় আর… এস,রেকর্ড দরখাস্তকারী'গন পূর্ববর্তী ও তাদের নামে আর,এস, ৯৯১ খতিয়ান প্রস্তুত হয়েছে৷ যদিও বাদীপ্ক্ষেনালিশী ভূমি প্রজাবিলিকৃত সম্পত্তি হিসাবে গেজেটভুক্ত নয় মর্মে দাবী করছেন৷ কিত্তু প্রজাবিলিসম্পত্তির পেজেট, নালিশীর ভূমির এস, এ ও আর,এস, খতিয়ান ২৮৪, ৯৯১ এবং ২৯ষ্০২-৫৬ ইংক্ষতিপুরন প্রদানের গেজেট সহ নথি পর্যালোচনারদেখা যায়, উল্লেখিত গেজেটে নালিশী ভূমির সি,এস খতিয়ান নং উল্লেখ না থাকলেও সি, এস মৌজাশুঞাবাদ, জে, এল ২৬০ উল্লেখে প্রকাশিত গেজেটনালিশী তুমি প্রজাবিলি সম্পত্তি হিসাবে গেজেট ভূজ্ঞহয়েছে এবং সেই প্রেক্ষিতে এস, এ ২৮৪ নংখতিয়ানে মন্তব্য কলামে ২৪/১ ধারা মতে এবংএরই ধারাবাহিকতায় আর, এস ৯৯১ নং খতিয়ান দরখাস্তকারীদের পূর্ববর্তী নাম উল্লেখ করাহয়েছে৷ সাবির্ক পর্যালোচনার নালিশী ভুমি প্রজাবিলিসম্পত্তি হিসাবে আদালতের কাছে প্রতীয়মান হওয়ায়এই নালিশী তুমি প্রজাব��লি সম্পত্তি সিদ্ধান্ত গ্রহনকরা হলো৷

সংশ্লিষ্ট আইনের বিধানাবলী, মহামান্য উচ্চআদালতের সিদ্ধান্ত, উভয় পক্ষের দরখাস্ত/লিখিতআপণ্ডিসহ নথি পর্যালোচনা করলাম৷ ইতোমধ্যেনালিশী তুমি প্ৰজাবিলিকৃত গেজেটভুক্ত সম্পত্তি মর্মে পর্যবক্ষন সহ সিদ্ধান্ত গ্রহন করা হয়েছে৷ যেহেতুনালিশী তুমি প্রজাবিলিকৃত গেজেটভুওন্ সম্পত্তি,তাই উল্লেখিত আইনের বিধানাবলী এবং মহামান্যউচ্চ আদালতের সিদ্ধান্তের আলোকে এই মোকদ্দমাটি সাক্ষ্য গ্রহনান্তে বিচারে দীর্ঘ্যয়িত না করে এই পর্যায়ে এবেট করা ন্যায় বিচার প্রসুত ওযুক্তিযুক্ত হবে বলে আদালত মন করেন৷ তাই উভয়পক্ষের শুনানী অন্তে ও গৃনাগুনের ভিত্তিতেদরখাস্তকারী ৰিবাদীপক্ষের আনীত দরখাস্ত মঞ্জুর মোকদ্দমাঢি এবেট/খারিজ হিসাবে গন্য করা হলো।”

9. Every court has inherent power for securing the ends of justice in any suit/proceeding; so, if the court from the admitted materials before it found that the pending litigation would ultimately brings no result in favour of the plaintiffs-petitioners can dismiss the suit/case before it; in that situation the court by exercising its inherent jurisdiction can dismiss or record the abatement of the suit or appeal; the inherent power has been approved by the precedents to the case of Abdul Jalil and others —vs- Islamic Bank Bangladesh Ltd. And others reported in 53 DLR(AD)-12, to the case of Sanatannessa Bewa —vs- Haipatullah Sarker & ors reported in 4] DLR(AD)-105;

10. In the present revisional application neither is deciding any disputed fact of issue or issues between the parties, rather, deciding the prayer of the defendants-opposite parties whether dismissing or abating the suit as per P.O. 90 of 1972 which is purely a question of law and the parties in suit being not are in issue rather are agreement with the acquisition of title by the plaintiff-petitioner in the projabili properties as cited in the projabili property Gaztte;

11. We have heard the submissions of the learned Advocates for the respective parties; we have also gone through the application for recording abatement (Annexure-"H") of the instant Revisional application. We further anxiously consider and perused the annexures appended with the revisional application; we have gone through P.O. 90 of 1972, referred precedents and the impugned judgment and order of the trial court with prayer of the suit;

On considering the submissions of the learned Advocates for both sides and perusing the annexures the question before us is whether the instant suit has been abated as per the provision of the P.O. No.90 of 1972, the precedents referred by the learned Advocate for the defendants-opposite parties and upon the observations and opinion of the trial court;

<u>12. In this connection we can place reliance to the following facts circumstances and laws which are necessary to dispose of the revisional application;</u>

<u>Before enactment of State Acquisition and Tenancy Act,1950 there was Zamindary system in the Land Administration and Zamindar had two types of property, one projabli property i.e. land settled to the projas subject to payment of rent to the Zamindar and another, the khas property i.e. the property remained in possession of the Zamindar like his own house, garden, ponds etc.; in the projabili property though the Zamindar had only rent receiving interest but in the name of realization of that interest/rent from the projas, the servants under the Zamindar (Paik Peyada) usually oppressed the projas/raiyats which shocked the then political leaders and social reformer for the abolishing the Zamindari System; those political leaders and social reformer promised to the general public that if the country is liberated from British Rule they would abolish the Zamindari system and the projas under the Zamindar won Id be upgraded as Malik (owner) from the status of Projas;</u>

13. Accordingly, when the India was divided in the year 1947, the erstwhile Government of the Province of East Pakistan for abolishing the Zamindari system declaring the projas rights and title as owners had enacted the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act in the year 1950;

14. The provincial Government for giving effect the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 in respect of the projabili property of the Za m i nda rim n t receiver remained under the management and custody of the court of Wards, the Government of the

Province of East Pakistan added Section 8(A) in the Court of Wards Act,1879 by the Act No. X of 1951 stating as Follows:

“If the rent-receiving interests of a ward in ally estate under the charge of the Court are acquired under the provisions of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, then, notwithstanding anything contained elsewhere in this Act, the remaining properties of such ward including khas lands under the charge and shall be managed in accordance with the provisions of this Act, as if such ward were a disqualified proprietor of an estate."

The Government as per said modes and procedure firstly published a gazette notification under Section 3 of the said SAT Act, 1950 Monday, March 24 in the year 1952 describing therein the description of the projabili property in the column No.2 and the name and address of the Zamindar/rent Receiver in the column No.1;

Before publication of the said projabili property gazette the Government had issued a notice under Section 3(A) and 4 of the said SAT Acti 950 asking the Zamindar/rent Receiver to submit a list of projabili property remained under possession of their projas and khas property under their ownership and possession with a penal clause in case of failure to submit the list as asked for;

The Government after receiving of the said list from the concerned Zamindar both of projabili and khan properties had published a projabili property gazette in the year 1952 under Section 3 of the said SAT Act,1950; so, the projas under the then Zamindar whether had/have no document connecting with the settlement as projas is very immaterial at least in deciding dispute in between the Zamindar and the projas;

15. After publication of the projabili property gazette under Section 3 by complying 3(A) and 4 of the said SAT Act, 950, the Government had done the following acts:

"(a) Assessed the compensation against acquiring the Zamindari/rent reciving interest of the Zamindar under Section 33 of the SAT Act,1950, which was commonly known as Compensation Assessment Roll (CA Roll);

(b)Thereafter paid the said assessed compensation as per CA Roll to the Zamindar;

©After payment of compensation to the Zamindar the then Government published a compensation payment gazette under Section 46E of the SAT Act, 1950 in the year 1956 declaring that the compensation assessed against acquisition of rent receiving interest of the Zamindar in the projabili property cited in the projabili property gazette published under Section 3 of the SAT Act, 1950 had been paid;

(d)After completing the aforesaid process for acquiring Zamindari/rent receiving interest, the property cited in the projabili property gazette were recorded in the names of concerned projas upgrading them as owners in the khatian as State Acquisition (S.A.) khatian after determining the land revenue under Section 24(1) of the SAT Act, 1950; as such, almost in all the S.A. Khatian 24(1) of the SAT Act, 1950 was cited as the basis of determining land development tax payable by the new owners by operation of law to the new rent receiver, the Government."

16. After the independence of Bangladesh when it found that the previous Zamindar were yet taking the advantage of loophole of the SAT Act, 1950 and thereby they were trying to make the said Act ineffective and meaningless by filing Civil Suits and other proceedings, Govt. of Bangladesh for protecting the projas who became owners by operation of law promulgated the President Oder No. 90 of 1972 prohibiting the previous projas whose properties had been cited in the projabili property gazette as follows:

"Notwithstanding anything contained in any other law for the time being in force, on the commencement of this Order, all suits, appeals, applications and other legal proceedings pending or continuing or deemed to be pending or continuing in any court against the Government of the People's Republic of Bangladesh in which the legality or validity of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act,1950 (E.B. Act No. XXVIII of 1951), or of the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy (Amendment) Ordinance, 1956 (E.B. Order No. Ill of 1956), or of any other law making any amendment in the said Act, or of the acquisition of any property made under any provision thereof is challenged or called in question shall abate, and all orders, including orders of injunction and other interlocutory orders, passed in such suits, appeals, applications and other legal proceedings shall cease to have any effect; and no court shall entertain, and no person shall bring, any fresh suit, appeal, application or other legal proceedings in which the legality or validity of any such law or acquisition is challenged or called in question."

17. In spite of the aforesaid amended Section 8(A) of the Court of Wards Act, 1879 and P.O. No. 90 of 1972, when the previous Zamindars were found in oppression to the previous projas, some of the previous projas who became owners by operation of law, went to the Supreme Court for redress wherein apex Court of the Country gave observations and directions to the previous Zmindars/rent receivers not to claiming any right, title and interest in their previous projabili properties.

18. In view of the aforementioned laws we can profitably place reliance further in the precedents to the case of Amara Begum Chowdhury VS Nur Jahan Begum and others reported in 27 DLR(SC) 109, Peoples Republic of Bangladesh Vs Sri Sri Madan Gopal Jew Bigraha and others reported in 33 DLR (AD) 13, and Promode Ranjan Saha and others Vs Govt. of Bangladesh and another reported in the said 33 DLR (AD) 53, Bhawal Raj Court of Wards Estate, represented by its Manager, Dhaka —Vs- Rasheda Begum and others reported in 14 MLR (AD) 401:

19. we are of the further view that the parties in the revision confronted with unnecessary and uncalled for litigations which has already been settled connecting with projabili property" by the apex Court in its decision to the case of People's Republic of Bangladesh represented by the Deputy Commissioner and others vs. Sri Sri Madan Gopal Jew Bigraha and others reported in 33 DLR (SC) wherein it has held that:

"It is now well settled on the authority of Jalil Ahmed vs. The province of East Pakistan decided by a Special Bench and affirmed by the Supreme Court of Pakistan in Tanvir Ahmed Siddiky vs. The province of East Pakistan that on the publication of the notification under section 3, of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, all rent-receiving interests including that of debater and waqf estate stood acquired and all non-retainable khas land also stood vested in the Government."

Further it has held that: P.O. 90 of 1972 puts a complete bar against any acquisition of any property by the Government-All suits. Etc. questioning acquisition shall stand abated.

In 1963 notifications u/s 43 of the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act have been published acquiring of all interests other than those already acquired under section 3 of the Act, completing all acquisition proceedings under the Act, All the notifications have been published in the official Gazette covering all districts of Bangladesh. Another fact is that the Bangladesh (Legal proceedings) (Third) Order, 1972 (P.O. 90 of 1972) was promulgated on 3rd August, 1972. This enactment has been promulgated long after all the acquisition proceedings were completed and long after the observations of the High Court made regarding the non-preparation of the compensation assessment-roll. President's Order No. 90 of 1972 clearly states that notwithstanding anything contained in any other law for the time being in force, on the commencement of this Order, all suits, appeals, applications, etc. pending in any court against the Government of Bangladesh in which the legality or validity of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, or of the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy (Amendment) Ordinance, 1956 (E.B . Ordinance No. III of 1956) or of any other law making any amendment in the said Act, or of the acquisition of any property make there under is challenged in question shall abate, and all orders, including orders of injunction, etc. passed in such suits, appeals, etc. shall cease to have any effect and no court shall entertain and no person shall bring any fresh suit, appeal, application or other legal proceedings in which the legality or validity of any such law or acquisition is challenged or called in question.

On the coming into operation of P.O. 90 of 1972 notwithstanding any other earlier view of law on the subject, a pending proceeding which is sought to be instituted challenging or calling in question the acquisition of any property under any of the provision of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act shall abate and no proceedings shall be instituted in any Court.

In the Case of Promode Ranjan Saha and others vs. Govt. of Bangladesh and another reported in 33 DLR (AD) 53 it has further held that:

After the promulgation of P.O 90 of 1972-all orders including orders of injunction etc. passed in appeals applications, etc. shall cease to have any effect and no court shall entertain any fresh suit, appeal, application, etc. in which the legality of any such law of acquisition is challenged or called in question."

"There can be no denying the fact that the plaintiffs sought to challenge the acquisition of their Hats and Bazars made under the general notification dated 02.04.1956 issued under section 3(1) of the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act and so it is directly hit by President's Order No. 90 of 1972, which gives a clear mandate of law that notwithstanding anything contained in any other law for the time being in force, on the commencement of this Order, all suits, appeals, applications and other legal proceedings pending or continuing in any Court against the Government of the People's Republic of Bangladesh in which the legality or validity of the State Acquisition and Tenancy (Amendment) Ordinance, 1956 (E.B. Ordinance No. III of 1956) or of any other law making any amendment in the said Act, or of the acquisition of any property made under any provision thereof is challenged or called in question shall abate and all orders, including orders of injunction and other interlocutory orders passed in such suits, appeals applications and other legal proceedings shall cease to have any effect; and no Court shall entered and no person shall bring any fresh suit, appeal, applications or other legal proceedings in which the legality or validity of any such law or acquisition is challenged or called in question."

20. We are of the view that it would be further noteworthy to refer to the precedent to the case of Abdul Jalil and others VERSUS Islamic Bank Bangladesh Ltd. And others reported in 53 DLR (AD) 12 wherein it has held that:

"As the ultimate result of the suit is as clear as day light such a suit should be buried at its inception so that no further time is consumed in a fruitless litigation."

21. Following the aforesaid law and decisions of the apex Court, the Hon'ble High Court Division in a case Sagufta M.N. Housing and others —vs- Government of Bangladesh and others reported in 19 MLR page-1, gave observations and directions as follows:

"With the publication of notification under section 3 all rent-receiving interests including wakf and debuttor property stood acquired by and vested in the Government   after the notification issued under section 3 of the Act the status of the under raiyat became upgraded to that of raiyat. The rent receiving interests can be acquired even before the preparation of record of rights and publication of compensation assessment rolls'..... with the acquisition of the rent-receiving interests by the government all intermediary interests disappeared and a person holding the land under the government becomes a "tenant", becomes a "raiyat" or a "malik" of the land recognized by the government.

Consequently, the under-raiyat who actually holds the land becomes a tenant under the government. Superior landlord (i.e. the raiyat) of an under-raiyat by virtue of elimination of his rent-receiving interests disappears and the under-raiyat is upgraded to the status of a "raiyat"..... record of rights prepared and finally published under section 144 cannot be varied by any authority and remedy of wrong entry if any lies with the appropriate court' the property enlisted/shown in the "projabili property" Gazette are the properties of "projas" who have been accepted as "owners" and the "rent-receiving" interest whatsoever it had therein, of the jamindars all vested to the Government by operation of SAT Act, 1950."

22. Therefore, from the above provisions of law and authorities we are of the considered view that the Court of Wards and land Reform Board, cannot, under any circumstances, claim ownership over the projabili property of the previous jamindar, Kumar Rabindra Narayan Ray Chowdhury and, as such, cannot proceed with suit/Revision in other words the suit in question has no legs to stand; however, even the petitioner by filing this revision trying to interfere with the right, title and interest of the opposite parties which are nothing but harassment who has become owners of their respective properties by operation of law;

23. It may mention here that in spite of the observations and directions given in the aforesaid judgments by our apex court when the court of Wards Bhawal Raj Estate again started to disturb its previous projas whose property has been cited in the projabili property gazette of 1952 by giving temporary lease to third parties, some of the owners by operation of law started several contempt proceedings before the Hon'ble High Court Division including the contempt proceedings Nos. 226 of 2013 and 346 of 2013; in those two contempt proceedings the Manager of Court of Wards Bhawal Raj Estate and the then Chairman, Land Reform Board appeared before the Hon'ble High Court Division and apologized unconditionally with undertaking that they would not further claim any sorts of right, title and interest over the projabili property as cited in the projabili property gazette dated 24.03.1952; a division bench of the Hon'ble High Court Division consisting of their lordships Mr. Justice Mirza Hussain Haidr and Mr. Justice Muhammad Khurshid Alam Sarker accepted their unconditional apology and disposed of the said contempt Rule with observations;

<u>24. It further appears that after aforesaid judgments of the apex Court, Government officer employed in the Government organization, the Court of Wards realized that the property of private incapable persons given for management and administration to the Court of Wards are not the property of the Court of Wards or Government and the Court of wards is a mere custodian or statutory attorney holder of the Wards, whose property was taken over for management and administration under the Court of Wards Act, 1879 and as such, the income whatever comes from those properties are the income of Wards and nothing of it is deposited in the Government exchequer; they also realized that they as a custodian of the Wards have no authority to retain in their khas possession firstly 375 Bighas thereafter 100 bighas and now 60 bighas; they further realized that they had so long done wrong acts, deeds and things in claiming over the projabili properties of the Court of Wards which vested to the projas as owners by operation of law;</u>

25. After realizing their mistake, they firstly issued a Circular No. 1 বি,আর,ঢাকা-৮১/২০০৩/৩২৪ dated 10.07.2014 under the signature of Mr. Raisuddin, the then Manager of Court of Wards, Bhawal Raj Estate cum Deputy Secretary of the Government of Bangladesh, stating that the Court of Wards as custodian of the Wards has no claim over the projabili properties as cited in the projabili property gazette published under Section 3 of the SAT Act, 1950 as per section 8(A) of the Court of Wards,1879 and P.O. 90 of 1972 and also the judgments passed by the apex Court; thereafter, the concerned Ministry of land had also issued a letter bearing Memo No. 31/00/0000/042/67.032/15 dated 27.10.2015 and another letter bearing Memo No. 31/00/0000/046/58/019.12-70 dated 02.02.2016 directing the Government servants engaged in the Court of Wards not to claim any sort of right, title and interest in the projabili property cited in the projabili property Gazette in 1952;

Since admittedly the property of the plaintiffs petitioner were projabili properties and accordingly cited the said property in the projabili property gazette dated 24.03.1952 at page 488 and since the Court of Wards was ordered to be refrained from claiming projabili property cited in the projabili property gazette by amended Section 8(A) of the Court of Wards Act, 1879 and P.O. 90 of 1972 and since following the aforesaid law, the Hon'ble Court as well as Appellate Division earlier had given direction not to claim any right, title and interest over the projabili property and if claimed by initiating any legal proceeding, then those to be abated; so, the impugned judgment and order No.140 dated 05.02.2014 passed by the Special Judge/Joint District and Sessions Judge of Paribesh Adalat, Dhaka in Title Suit No. 1932 of 2008 according to the aforesaid facts, circumstances and laws and the decisions of the apex Court have been cited hereinabove cannot be proceeded and interfered by this court;

26. It is admitted facts that at the time of the abolishing the Zamindari system the erstwhile East Pakistan Government had enacted the law namely: "The East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act in the year 1950."

In pursuance of that law there was a clear direction upon the Zamindar to submit a report stating the description of the property which they would like to stay as their khas properties and what property would be the land of tenant i.e. projabili;

As per Dacca Gazette dated 24th March 1952 the suit C.S. Khatian 63 and 66 were in the part of Projabili property of Kumar Rabindra Narayan Ray Chowdhury;

So, it is also revealed and admitted that the suit property was not khas property of the Jamindar, rather, admittedly it was projabili property; So, diming plaintiff declaration of right, titles and recovery of khas possession over the suit land are none but the Court of Wards is clearly against the provision of law of the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act in as much as under the provision of P.O. 90 of 1972 as the suit C.S. Khatian No. 63 and 66.

27. We have gone through the case law as well as the provision of law of East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act and P.O. No. 90 of 1972 as cited herein above then it would be crystal clear that the plaintiff filed the suit by challenging the acquisition of the property as a Court of Wards in violation of the provisions of Sections (3) A, 4 of the SAT Act-1950 read with Section 8(A) of the Court of Wards Act,1879 (Act No. X of 1951) and P.O. 90 of 1972.

28. Although we are not deciding the suit on merit but we cannot over looked the above legal and lawful consequence for which at the ends of the hearing of the suit will not give any fruit for the plaintiff petitioner rather it is crystal clear as per legal provisions of law as cited above the suit in question is nothing but a fruitless litigation.

29. On perusal of the application under Section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure (Annexure-"H" to the revisional application). It appears that the cause shown or explanations made were very much clear. In paragraph Nos. 2-5 of the said application it is stated that how the suit barred by law and on examination of decision, reason and conclusion reached by learned Special Judge it cannot be at all said that those are tainted with patent illegality, legal infirmity and flagrant error of law warranting any interference by this court in the exercise of civil revisional authority under Section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure.

30. We are, therefore, of the considered view that according to aforementioned facts, circumstances, laws, and the precedents of the apex Court as have been cited hereinabove and the discussions made hereinabove there is no doubt rather it is crystal clear that the suit clearly abated in accordance with law as the petitioners have no locus standi to proceed with the suit.

31. In that view of the matter we find no merit in the revisional application. Therefore we are not inclined to restore the revisional application which was discharged for default on 05.06.2016.

32. Resultantly, the restoration application dated 05.06.2016 is rejected without any order as to cost;

The office is directed to communicate this order to the court below at once.

End.
